 Run `python3 -m unittest` and `python3 bench.py` from this directory.
 
+
+## Import throughput estimate
+
+`python3 bench.py` creates 200 batches of 1,000 valid JSON lines before
+starting the timers, then calls `feed.import_lines` on each batch in turn.
+Each call parses and validates the lines, inserts distinct IDs into a fresh
+file-backed SQLite database, and commits the batch. The benchmark verifies
+that all 200,000 records were accepted and stored. It reports the mean and
+slowest batch times and their margins against the one second available for
+1,000 records at 1,000 records/second. It runs batches without pacing; 200,000
+records represent 200 seconds of incoming data, not a 200-second endurance
+run. Database creation, input generation, the final count, and cleanup are not
+included in the reported processing times.
+
+The estimate uses short, valid records, an initially empty database growing to
+200,000 rows, a 1,000-record transaction, SQLite defaults, and the filesystem
+of the current directory. Results do not include feed acquisition, queueing,
+other application work, long-term growth, or contention. Different record
+sizes, invalid data, transaction sizes, database sizes, or storage can change
+the result. In particular, a memory-backed filesystem cannot measure
+persistent-storage commit latency. Run the benchmark on the intended storage
+and with representative input and load before relying on it in production.
